  DESCRIPTIVE C0 (3/3): first mover won 12/20 (60%, 95% CI 39%–78%, exact p vs 50% = 0.503). The interval includes 50%: no reliable first-mover effect at n = ${c.games}. Mean length 16.8 turns (95% CI 7.998–25.602), turn-limit rate 15%.
  DESCRIPTIVE C1 (2/2): first mover won 13/20 (65%, 95% CI 43%–82%, exact p vs 50% = 0.263). The interval includes 50%: no reliable first-mover effect at n = ${c.games}. Mean length 9.45 turns (95% CI 7.619–11.281), turn-limit rate 0%.
  DESCRIPTIVE C2 (4/4): first mover won 12/20 (60%, 95% CI 39%–78%, exact p vs 50% = 0.503). The interval includes 50%: no reliable first-mover effect at n = ${c.games}. Mean length 18.9 turns (95% CI 11.99–25.81), turn-limit rate 10%.
  DESCRIPTIVE C3 (3/2): first mover won 10/20 (50%, 95% CI 30%–70%, exact p vs 50% = 1). The interval includes 50%: no reliable first-mover effect at n = ${c.games}. Mean length 15.7 turns (95% CI 8.419–22.981), turn-limit rate 10%.
  DESCRIPTIVE C3: the side that started with more pieces won 10/20 (50%, 95% CI 30%–70%, exact p vs 50% = 1). More pieces also means more actions per turn and a larger victory requirement, so this is the effect of the whole package, not of piece count alone.
  SIGNAL      C4 (2/3): first mover won 17/20 (85%, 95% CI 64%–95%, exact p vs 50% = 0.003). The interval excludes 50%: an exploratory first-mover signal, to be re-tested on fresh seeds. Mean length 15.85 turns (95% CI 8.498–23.202), turn-limit rate 10%.
  SIGNAL      C4: the side that started with more pieces won 3/20 (15%, 95% CI 5%–36%, exact p vs 50% = 0.003). More pieces also means more actions per turn and a larger victory requirement, so this is the effect of the whole package, not of piece count alone.
  SIGNAL      All 100 games pooled: first mover won 64/100 (64%, 95% CI 54%–73%, exact p vs 50% = 0.007). Pooled across different rules, so it only says whether a first-mover tendency shows up broadly.
  NO_EVIDENCE Do the five conditions differ in first-mover win rate? Permutation test p = 0.243. No evidence that starting piece count changes first-mover advantage at this sample size. This is absence of evidence, not evidence of no effect.
  DESCRIPTIVE C1 vs C0 on the same seed and first mover: first-mover outcome flipped toward the first mover in 5 pairs and away in 4 (exact sign test p = 1). RNG trajectories: 1/20 games never diverged from the reference RNG sequence, so pairs share a seed but not a game.
  DESCRIPTIVE C2 vs C0 on the same seed and first mover: first-mover outcome flipped toward the first mover in 4 pairs and away in 4 (exact sign test p = 1). RNG trajectories: 1/20 games never diverged from the reference RNG sequence, so pairs share a seed but not a game.
  DESCRIPTIVE C3 vs C0 on the same seed and first mover: first-mover outcome flipped toward the first mover in 3 pairs and away in 5 (exact sign test p = 0.727). RNG trajectories: 0/20 games never diverged from the reference RNG sequence, so pairs share a seed but not a game.
  DESCRIPTIVE C4 vs C0 on the same seed and first mover: first-mover outcome flipped toward the first mover in 8 pairs and away in 3 (exact sign test p = 0.227). RNG trajectories: 0/20 games never diverged from the reference RNG sequence, so pairs share a seed but not a game.
  DESCRIPTIVE Game length (mean turns): C0 16.8, C1 9.45, C2 18.9, C3 15.7, C4 15.85. Fewer pieces means fewer actions per turn but also fewer pieces to bring across.
  DESCRIPTIVE Conveyor-belt moves (a piece stepping on its own side's trap and being carried forward): C0 14.65/game (more-conveyor side won 10/18); C1 11.3/game (more-conveyor side won 11/20); C2 17.45/game (more-conveyor side won 8/19); C3 12.65/game (more-conveyor side won 11/18); C4 13.35/game (more-conveyor side won 11/18). An association with winning is not proof that conveyors cause wins: stronger positions also create more chances to use them.
  LIMITATION  Both sides use the same deterministic planner and weights, so "Human side" and "AI side" are board directions, not different players. Results describe this planner playing itself, not human play.
  LIMITATION  These 100 games are exploratory. They were looked at to form hypotheses and cannot also serve as the holdout that tests them.
